            <ArticleTitle>Interventional Removal of Knotted Pulmonary Artery Catheter: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>885066617733933</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/0885066617733933</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 60-year-old female patient underwent emergency cadaveric liver transplantation. During the insertion of the pulmonary artery catheter (PAC) through the left innominate vein, advancement of the catheter was not successful and a knot occurred. An interventional radiologist performed antegrade rigid wire insertion into the PAC and succeeded in loosening the knot in the vessel. We report the successful removal of a knot through intervention without additional invasive procedures in a hemodynamically unstable patient with a coagulopathy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
